To fix the issue where `infoWindow.close()` does not close other `InfoWindows` on click when using the Google Maps API, you should manage the opened `InfoWindow` instance properly. As it stands, your current implementation initializes a new `InfoWindow` for each marker, which leads to the `infoWindow.close()` command not effectively closing any previously opened window.

Here's how you can modify your existing code:

1. Keep track of the currently open `InfoWindow` by setting it to a variable (in your case, `openInfoWindow`). Only close it if it is already open when another marker is clicked.

2. Update your click listener to close the previously opened `InfoWindow` before opening a new one.

Here is an example of how this can be achieved:

```javascript
function initMap(obj, error) {
    if (error) { console.log("Error: " + error) }

    var openInfoWindow = null;

    var mapProp = {
        center: { lat: 39.8283, lng: -98.5795 },
        zoom: 2
    };
    var map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById("googleMap"), mapProp);

    for (var i = 0; i < obj.length; i++) {
        var latLng = new google.maps.LatLng(obj[i].lat, obj[i].lng);
        var marker = new google.maps.Marker({
            position: latLng,
            map: map
        });

        var contentStr = '<div id="popcontent">' +
            '<h1 id="firstHeading" class="firstHeading">' + obj[i].description + '</h1>' +
            '<p>' + obj[i].location + ', ' + obj[i].con + '</p>' +
            '</div>';

        var infoWindow = new google.maps.InfoWindow({
            maxWidth: 300
        });

        google.maps.event.addListener(marker, 'click', (function(marker, contentStr, infoWindow) {
            return function() {
                // Close the currently open InfoWindow, if there is one
                if (openInfoWindow) {
                    openInfoWindow.close();
                }

                // Set the new content and open the InfoWindow
                infoWindow.setContent(contentStr);
                infoWindow.open(map, marker);

                // Update the reference to the currently open InfoWindow
                openInfoWindow = infoWindow;
            };
        })(marker, contentStr, infoWindow));
    }
}
```

In this modified version, when a marker is clicked:
- Before opening a new `InfoWindow`, it checks if `openInfoWindow` is not null. If it is open, it closes it.
- It then opens the new `InfoWindow` and updates `openInfoWindow` to the one that was just opened. 

This way, you ensure that only one `InfoWindow` is open at a time, addressing the problem you described [SO-11121128].

To reliably determine if a browser supports mouseover events, you can use a combination of feature detection techniques to distinguish between devices that support mouse events and those that rely solely on touch events. Here’s a clear resolution based on the context provided.

1. **Detect Mouse Presence**: You can check for mouse capabilities by listening for mouse movement events. If a `mousemove` event is detected, you can assume that the device has mouse input capabilities.

2. **Detect Touchscreen Presence**: Similarly, you can check for touch support by listening for touch events. If a `touchstart` event is detected, you can infer that the device is primarily a touch-based device.

Here’s a sample code based on your provided details:

```javascript
jQuery(function() {
    // Detects if the device has mouse capabilities
    jQuery("body").one("mousemove", function(e) {
        attachMouseEvents(); // Function to attach mouse-related events
    });

    // Detects if the device has touchscreen capabilities
    jQuery("body").one("touchstart", function(e) {
        // Unbind the mouse detector to prevent false positives
        jQuery("body").unbind("mousemove");
        attachTouchEvents(); // Function to attach touch-related events
    });
});
```

In this code:
- The `one("mousemove", ...)` event handler will trigger if the user has a mouse, thus allowing you to attach mouse-related events via `attachMouseEvents()`.
- The `one("touchstart", ...)` event handler will trigger if the device supports touch, at which point you unbind the mouse event to avoid misfiring, and then call `attachTouchEvents()` to handle touch-related actions.

This improves detection reliability and gives a clear separation between devices that can handle mouse and touch interactions effectively [SO-4643443][SO-8935287][SO-2788799].

