1. **Set Up Your HTML and JavaScript**:
   You already have the structure in place for your HTML and JavaScript. You will modify your JS to add an event listener to the document that checks for clicks outside the navigation links.

2. **Modify Your JavaScript**:
   You need to add a function that will close your menu when a click outside is detected. This can be integrated as shown below:

```javascript
const burger = document.querySelector(".burger");
const navLinks = document.querySelector(".header-nav-links");
const links = document.querySelectorAll(".header-nav-links li");

//Toggle Nav
burger.addEventListener("click", () => {
    navLinks.classList.toggle("open");

    //Animate Links
    links.forEach((link, index) => {
        if (link.style.animation) {
            link.style.animation = "";
        } else {
            link.style.animation = `navLinkFade 0.5s ease forwards ${index / 7 + 0.2}s`;
        }
    });
});

// Close the menu when clicking outside
document.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
    if (!navLinks.contains(event.target) && !burger.contains(event.target)) {
        navLinks.classList.remove("open");
        links.forEach((link) => {
            link.style.animation = "";
        });
    }
});
```

3. **Explanation of the Code**:
   - We listen for a click event on the entire document. Inside the event handler, we check if the clicked target is not within the navigation links (`navLinks`) or the burger menu (`burger`). If it is outside, we remove the `open` class from `navLinks`, which effectively closes the menu.
   - The use of `navLinks.contains(event.target)` checks if the clicked target is one of the menu items or part of the navigation, preventing the menu from closing when clicking inside it [SO-42201156].

4. **CSS Handling**:
   Ensure that your CSS handles the `.open` class to display or hide the navigation links accordingly. For example:
```css
.header-nav-links.open {
    display: block; // Or whatever property you use to show the menu
}
```

